<article class="env-lemma" id="0G8S">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0G8S">Lemma <span data-tag="0G8S">10.70.6</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $I = (a_1, \ldots , a_ n)$ be an ideal of $R$. Let $a = a_1$. Then there is a surjection </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  R[x_2, \ldots , x_ n]/(a x_2 - a_2, \ldots , a x_ n - a_ n) \longrightarrow \textstyle {R[\frac{I}{a}]}  \]
</div>
  <p> whose kernel is the $a$-power torsion in the source. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Consider the ring map $P = \mathbf{Z}[t_1, \ldots , t_ n] \to R$ sending $t_ i$ to $a_ i$. Set $J = (t_1, \ldots , t_ n)$. By Example <a href="/tag/0G8R" data-tag="0G8R">10.70.5</a> we have $P[\frac{J}{t_1}] = P[x_2, \ldots , x_ n]/(t_1 x_2 - t_2, \ldots , t_1 x_ n - t_ n)$. Apply Lemma <a href="/tag/0BIP" data-tag="0BIP">10.70.3</a> to the map $P \to A$ to conclude. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>